    "narrative": "The offensive engine for LawDog today is anchored by **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, who boasts an elite score of 79.71 against Montero. The combination of an advantageous 4.51 xERA opposing starter and the +8% boost from batting second creates a high-floor, high-ceiling scenario. **Daulton Varsho** (63.66) and **Will Smith** (65.60) also profile as strong starts. **Varsho** in particular benefits from a favorable wind projection (+5%) and a solid platoon edge, while **Will Smith** is positioned to capitalize on a mid-rotation arm in Canning, maintaining his status as our primary C1.\n\nWe are navigating several marginal starts, most notably **Gavin Sheets** (41.89) and **Jonathan Aranda** (46.30). **Sheets** is trending toward the bottom of our efficiency threshold; while his platoon advantage is active, he faces Sheehan\u2019s 3.18 xERA, which significantly suppresses his outlook. The most difficult bench decision involved **Taylor Ward**, who finished at 39.38. Despite the benefit of leading off, his score was dragged down by a poor park factor and a difficult matchup against Jax, causing him to narrowly miss the 40-point threshold required to displace players like **Drew Gilbert** or **Gavin Sheets**. \n\nRisk management remains paramount today given the number of starters facing pitchers with xERAs near or below 3.00. **Bryson Stott** (53.36) faces the elite arm of Ch. Burns; while the wind helps his outlook, he is a primary candidate for a mid-game swap if his efficiency stalls. As we monitor the wire, keep an eye on the health status of **Cal Raleigh**, who remains sidelined on the IL, and ensure that reserves like **Masyn Winn**, **Brendan Donovan**, and **Jung Hoo Lee** are ready to rotate in should the late-afternoon lineups shift. We continue to prioritize volume, but with SB being the scarce commodity, we are keeping a close watch on the sprint speeds of any late-inning defensive replacements who could steal a bag.",
